Make testFeature a no-op when the .feature file is missing.

Required by haptic's downstream sharding harness
(tests/conformance/ingress_sharding.go), which materialises only the
subset of upstream feature files this shard owns into a per-shard
features/ dir, then runs the upstream binary against it. Without this
patch the upstream TestSuite iterates over its hardcoded `features`
map of paths, calls godog.TestSuite{}.Run() for each, and any missing
file produces "feature path X is not available" + exit code != 0 —
which fails the whole suite even though every scenario that DID run
on this shard passed.

Treating a missing path as a no-op preserves the semantic the
hardcoded `features` map encodes (run every feature the binary knows
about) for the unsharded path (all files present) while allowing
sharded callers to omit non-owned files. Same conditional approach
upstream itself would need if it ever grew support for partitioned
runs.

Apply with `git apply` from the upstream checkout root. Pinned to
commit d920ed36a0076e169a9a329a850844ab3a695ae8 — bumping that SHA in
the Makefile may require re-resolving this patch.

--- a/conformance_test.go
+++ b/conformance_test.go
@@ -131,6 +131,15 @@ func TestSuite(t *testing.T) {
 }

 func testFeature(feature string, scenarioInitializer func(*godog.ScenarioContext)) error {
+	// Sharded runs (driven by external orchestration that materialises
+	// only the feature files this shard owns) may omit some files from
+	// the hardcoded `features` map. Treat a missing path as a no-op
+	// rather than failing the whole suite on "feature path not
+	// available" — vendored downstream by the haptic ingress-conformance
+	// sharding harness (tests/conformance/ingress_sharding.go).
+	if _, err := os.Stat(feature); os.IsNotExist(err) {
+		return nil
+	}
 	var testOutput io.Writer
 	// default output is stdout
 	testOutput = os.Stdout
